"Praise belongs to Allah, who created the heavens and the earth, and made darkness and light. His sovereignty and dominion encompass not only the subsequent states of beings, but also creating and bringing them into existence. The expressions of praise that occur at the beginning of five surahs in the Quran are explanations of the general praise in al-Fatihah from various perspectives.\n\nIn this verse, the verb 'khalq' (creation) is used for the heavens and the earth, while 'ja'l' (making/appointing) is used for darkness and light. While 'khalq' carries the meaning of bringing something into existence out of nothing and ordaining it, 'ja'l' is more general and involves relating something to another thing or transforming it—such as 'making the night a time for rest.'\n\nThe creation of darkness and light is in respect of their being opposite phenomena that succeed one another. This does not indicate the existence of two separate deities as the Magians claim; on the contrary, these are secondary and relative phenomena. The fact that 'zulumat' (darkness) appears in the plural while 'nur' (light) is singular points to the difference between shirk (polytheism) and tawhid (monotheism). Furthermore, these concepts are also used metaphorically in spiritual meanings, such as disbelief and faith, or ignorance and knowledge.\n\nAllah initially created human beings from clay. Fakhr al-Din al-Razi states that every human being indirectly originates from clay because nourishment is derived from the soil. However, according to the well-known tafsir, this refers to the creation of Adam. The creation of human beings from semen is also from a base fluid, yet the origin of semen ultimately traces back to the soil.\n\nModern science bases the origin of life on the cell. It is impossible for the cell to arise on its own (spontaneous generation); this necessitates the existence of a divine Creator. Pasteur's experiments also demonstrated that living beings reproduce only through seeds of their own kind. The creation of the first cell from earthly matter is proof of Allah's power.\n\nThere is an appointed term (lifespan) for every human being. Two appointed terms are mentioned in this verse: one is the term that is decreed (which actually takes place), and the other is the term determined in Allah's presence (the specified term, ajal musamma). This second term is the Day of Resurrection and Reckoning. Some commentators have interpreted these two terms differently; however, the correct view is the distinction between the appointed time of death and the appointed time of the Hereafter.\n\nAllah is the only deity worshipped in the heavens and on the earth; He knows what is secret and what is open. The disbelievers turn away from the verses/signs of their Lord and deny the truth (the Quran). However, news of what they used to ridicule will surely come to them.\n\nWhen narrating the destruction of previous nations, Allah Almighty uses the word 'qarn'. Qarn signifies a community living in a particular epoch. Peoples such as 'Ad and Thamud were destroyed because of their sins despite the immense power and blessings granted to them. This demonstrates that sins are a significant factor in bringing about a nation's appointed end. If Allah wills, He can destroy you in a similar manner and bring forth others in your place.\n\nThe polytheists demanded of the Prophet Muhammad that a tangible book be sent down. However, Allah stated that even if such a miracle were to come, they would say, 'This is nothing but obvious magic.' The verse revealed upon this demand of Abdullah b. Abi Umayyah shows that those who persist in denial will reject even the clearest proofs. Their true intention is not to believe in the truth.\n\nThe polytheists demanded that an angel be sent down, saying, 'Why was an angel not sent down to him?!' But Allah declared that in such a case the matter would be settled, and that they would be unable to bear seeing an angel and would perish. For the original form of angels is terrifying beyond human endurance. Even the prophets could rarely see angels in their original form. For this reason, Allah sent His prophets as human beings.\n\nIf the messenger had been an angel, We would have sent him in human form. Even then, the polytheists would have continued to deny him, saying, 'You are just a human being like us.' Indeed, previous messengers were also ridiculed, and in the end, punishment encompassed those who mocked. Therefore, the Prophet Muhammad was commanded to travel through the land and see the end of those who denied the truth.\n\nAllah declares that whatever is in the heavens and on the earth belongs to Him. He has prescribed mercy upon Himself. That is, He has made mercy His attribute; His mercy takes precedence over His wrath. He will gather everyone on the Day of Resurrection; yet those who have ruined their own souls (the disbelievers) do not believe in this. Whatever rests in the night and the day belongs to Him; He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad is instructed not to take any ally other than Allah. Allah is the Creator of the heavens and the earth; He feeds, but is not fed. The Prophet was commanded to be the first of those who submit to Islam and was told not to be of the polytheists. If he were to disobey, he feared the torment of a mighty Day. Whoever is spared from the torment on that Day has truly attained mercy. If Allah afflicts you with harm, none can remove it but He; and if He bestows good upon you, He has power over all things. He is the Supreme over His servants, the All-Wise, the All-Aware.\n\nThe word 'Fatir' means to cleave or originate something for the first time. The creation of the heavens and the earth was such that non-existence was cleaved open and existence emerged. Fitrah is the initial state of creation, while nature (tabi'ah) is the harmony in the continuation of this state. Allah both creates the innate dispositions (fitrahs) and provides the necessary provisions for their continuation.\n\nThe polytheists demanded of the Prophet Muhammad that he produce a witness to his prophethood. Allah declared that He Himself is the greatest witness. The Quran was revealed to warn both the people of Mecca and everyone who comes after. The Prophet bears witness to the oneness of Allah and is free from what they associate with Him. Those who were given the Scripture (Jews and Christians) recognize the Prophet just as they recognize their own children; yet a group among them knowingly denies it. Who is more unjust than one who invents a lie against Allah or denies His verses/signs? On that Day, the polytheists will lie, saying, 'By our Lord, we were not polytheists,' and the idols they fabricated will vanish from them.\n\nWithout Allah's testimony, nothing can testify to its own existence. The existence and knowledge of everything rely on Allah's knowledge. Even the testimony in one's conscience is in fact a reflection of Allah's testimony. For this reason, certain knowledge can only be attained through Allah's testimony. Prophethood is likewise a special testimony and command of Allah; the prophethood of Muhammad (peace be upon him) is established through the testimony that Allah placed in his heart.\n\nWhat was fundamental to Muhammad (peace be upon him) was not self-confidence, but trust in Allah. He would say, 'I commit my affair to Allah,' and always relied upon Allah. To rely on one's ego (nafs) is to fail to recognize Allah and to make oneself a deity. This state of the Prophet is proof of his prophethood. Contrary to the claim of the Italian historian, the Prophet did not rely on himself, but placed his trust in Allah (tawakkul).\n\nThe hearts of those who listen to the Quran are veiled; these veils prevent them from understanding it. They describe the Quran as 'tales of the ancients.' Figures like Abu Sufyan, Walid, and Nadr listened to the Prophet, but did not accept the truth because of the barriers over their hearts. These barriers are formed through a person's own evil choices and persist by Allah's leave.\n\nWhile the word 'asatir' originally meant 'written things', over time it came to be used in the sense of 'legends, tales'. Those who characterized the Quran as 'asatir al-awwalin' viewed it not as a divine book, but as tales of the ancients. Yet the Quran is a miracle that conveys truths. This characterization stems from the corruption of their hearts.\n\nWhen the disbelievers witness the punishment of the Fire, they feel regret and wish to return to the world. However, this regret does not stem from genuine faith, but from desperation. Even if they were returned to the world, they would commit the very same wrongdoing again. The wrongdoers who fabricate lies against Allah and deny His verses can never attain salvation. The life of the Hereafter is far better than the life of this world; one who possesses reason chooses the Hereafter.\n\nIn these verses, Allah Almighty declares that while the disbelievers deny the resurrection after death and the Day of Judgment, when they find themselves in distress they show remorse, yet if they were sent back they would do the very same things, and they are liars in these words of theirs. In this world, they found themselves in distress many times and showed remorse, but at the very first opportunity they returned to their former evil ways. When cast into the Fire in the Hereafter, they will say, 'Oh, would that we were sent back, so that we would not deny the verses of our Lord and would be among the believers!' Yet this remorse is not sincere; for they are only perturbed when the evil deeds they previously concealed are cast in their faces. Otherwise, if they were sent back, they would again do the very things they were forbidden from doing.\n\nDisbelief and rebellion have become such an ingrained disposition for them that they can neither turn back nor be turned back. When they are brought before their Lord, Allah will say to them, 'Was this resurrection after death not real?' and they will reply, 'Yes, by our Lord, it was real.' Allah will say, 'Then taste the punishment, because you used to deny it.' Those who denied the meeting with Allah have suffered loss. Loss (khusran) is the forfeit of immense reward and everlasting bliss. Burden (wizr) and liability (wabal) are the weight of major sins. Humans have been granted intellect, senses, and faculties so that with them they may attain the realities of the afterlife; if one uses them merely for worldly pleasures, at the end of life they will suffer an irreparable loss.\n\nThe life of this world is nothing but amusement and play. Its fleeting pleasures mean nothing to those who contemplate death. The abode of the Hereafter, however, is better for those mindful of Allah (taqwa); for it is enduring and eternal. Allah invites people to reflect by saying, 'Will you not reason?' Because the disbelievers assume worldly life to be the sole reality, they plunge into heedlessness, chase after diversion, and deny the verses of Allah.\n\nAs a consolation to the Messenger of Allah, it is stated: 'Do not let their words grieve you; for in truth, they do not deny you, but rather they reject the verses of Allah.' Polytheists such as Abu Jahl and al-Harith ibn 'Amir admitted that the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) was truthful, yet out of obstinacy they did not believe. This stems from their denial of Allah and His power. The messengers before you were also denied and persecuted, yet they remained patient. The help of Allah came to them, and His promises never change. Indeed, some of the news of the messengers has already come to you.\n\nIf the turning away of the disbelievers weighs heavily upon you, then even if you could find a tunnel into the earth or a ladder into the sky to bring them a sign, what good would it do? Had Allah willed, He would have gathered them all upon guidance. So do not be among the ignorant! Only those who listen accept the call. As for the dead, Allah resurrects them, and then to Him they are returned. The polytheists said, 'Why has a sign not been sent down to him from his Lord?' Say, 'Indeed, Allah is fully capable of sending down a sign, but most of them do not know.' Just as they fail to benefit from the verses that have been sent down, they would not benefit from a new miracle either.\n\nThe animals that walk upon the earth and the birds that fly with their wings are communities like you. We have not neglected anything in the Book; then they will all be gathered before their Lord. Those who deny Our verses are deaf and dumb, lingering in darkness. Allah leads astray whom He wills, and places whom He wills upon a straight path. Say, 'Tell me: if the punishment of Allah should come upon you or the Hour should arrive, would you call upon anyone other than Allah? Answer, if you are truthful!'\n\nIn moments of distress and dire necessity, the disbelievers, by virtue of their innate nature (fitrah), pray to Allah alone and forget all others besides Him. If Allah wills, He removes that distress, whereupon they revert once again to their former polytheism. This state of affairs demonstrates that man turns sincerely to Allah in times of hardship, yet becomes ungrateful once ease is attained.\n\nMessengers were also sent to earlier communities, and when they rejected them, they were afflicted with hardship and distress. In this way, they were expected to supplicate humbly and repent. However, they grew obstinate and hardened their hearts; and Satan made what they did appear pleasing to them. In the end, when they forgot the warnings, Allah opened the gates of everything to them, and amidst abundance and prosperity they became arrogant and fell into heedlessness. Just as they were rejoicing and relishing their pleasures, they were seized suddenly by the punishment and plunged into despair. Thus, the root of the people who committed wrongdoing was cut off; and praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds.\n\nThe Messenger of Allah is commanded to say to the polytheists: 'Tell me: if Allah were to take away your hearing and your sight and seal your hearts, what deity other than Allah could restore them to you?' The verses are explained in varied ways, yet they still turn away. Ask again: 'If the punishment of Allah should come upon you suddenly or openly, would anyone be destroyed except the wrongdoing people?'\n\nMessengers are sent only as bearers of glad tidings and as warners. Whoever believes and performs righteous deeds, there shall be no fear upon them, nor shall they grieve. As for those who deny Our verses, they will be afflicted with punishment because of the wickedness they committed. The Messenger does nothing except follow what is revealed to him; the treasures of Allah are not in his possession, nor does he know the unseen (ghayb), nor is he an angel. He only warns with the Quran those who fear being gathered before their Lord.\n\nDo not dismiss from your presence those who worship their Lord morning and evening, seeking His pleasure. You are not responsible for their reckoning in the least, nor are they responsible for yours. If you dismiss them, you will be among the wrongdoers. Thus We have tested people through one another, so that they say, 'Has Allah favored these among us?' Does Allah not best know those who are grateful? When those who believe in Our verses come to you, greet them with peace; your Lord has prescribed mercy upon Himself: whoever among you does evil out of ignorance and then repents and amends, He is indeed Forgiving, Merciful. Thus do We explain the verses in detail, so that the way of the criminals may become manifest.\n\nAccording to the narration, when the notables of Quraysh saw poor Muslims such as Suhayb, Bilal, 'Ammar, and Salman alongside the Prophet (peace be upon him), they said, 'Are we to follow behind these people? Drive them away from your presence so that we may speak with you.' The Messenger of Allah replied, 'I will not drive the believers away.' Umar said, 'Why not try it and see what happens?' Upon their insistence, this verse was revealed. Later, the verse in Surah al-Kahf was revealed: 'And keep yourself patient with those who call upon their Lord morning and evening,' and the Prophet (peace be upon him) never separated them from his side, saying, 'Life is with you, and death is with you.'\n\nIn the 53rd verse, it is explained that Allah tests people by means of one another by blessing the poor and weak with faith and sincerity. Those seized by arrogance and pride say, 'Did Allah prefer these over us?' This trial exposes the attitudes of those who pride themselves on wealth and status toward poor and weak believers. Those who abandon their pride and repent succeed, whereas those who persist in obstinacy lose. In this way, the underlying wisdom behind the prohibition of driving away the believers is elucidated.\n\nIn verses 54-55, it is given as glad tidings that peace should be conveyed to those who believe in Our verses, that your Lord has prescribed mercy upon Himself, and that whoever commits evil out of ignorance and then repents and reforms will be forgiven. Allah explains the verses in this way so that the path of the criminals may become clearly evident.\n\nIn verses 56-59, the Prophet is informed that he is forbidden to worship anything other than Allah and that he will not follow their desires. It is said, 'I am upon clear proof from my Lord, yet you have denied it. The punishment you seek to hasten is not in my hands; the judgment belongs only to Allah.' With Him are the keys of the unseen; in the land and the sea, He knows the falling of a leaf, the grain in the darkness of the earth, and everything wet and dry, in a clear book (al-Lawh al-Mahfuz).\n\nIn verses 60-62, it is explained that Allah puts people to sleep at night (takes their souls), knows what they do by day, and then raises and awakens them. When the moment of death arrives, Our messengers take the soul, and then they are returned to their true Master (Mawla). The judgment belongs to Him, and He is the swiftest of reckoners. Here, an analogy is drawn between sleep and death; waking up each night is evidence for the resurrection in the Hereafter.\n\nIn verses 63-65, it is stated that it is Allah who delivers them from the darknesses of the land and the sea, yet even though people implore Him and promise to be grateful, they still associate partners with Him. Allah has the power to send punishment from above (celestial disasters) and from beneath (terrestrial disasters), and to divide people into sects and make them taste the violence of one another. The verses are explained in this way so that they may understand.\n\nIn verses 66-67, it is stated that although the people denied the Quran, it is the truth, and for every news there is a determined time. The Prophet is not a guardian over them, but merely a messenger.\n\nIn verses 68-70, it is commanded: when you see those who engage in idle mockery of Our verses, turn away from them, and do not sit with the wrongdoers. Leave those who take their religion as play and amusement and are deceived by the worldly life; but remind with the Quran so that no soul is surrendered to ruin for what it has earned, for it has no protector or intercessor besides Allah. For those who disbelieve, there will be a boiling drink and a painful punishment.\n\nIn verses 71-73, it is stated that turning away from beseeching things besides Allah that can neither benefit nor harm is required, and that turning back from the right path is like becoming someone bewildered whom devils have led astray. Allah's way is the only right path; we have been commanded to establish prayer and fear Allah. It is He who created the heavens and the earth in truth; whenever He says to a thing 'Be,' it is. On the day the Trumpet (Sur) is blown, the sovereignty belongs to Him; He knows the unseen and the witnessed.\n\nIn this section, the struggle of Abraham (Ibrahim) with his people is recounted. Allah proves the belief in Tawhid (the oneness of Allah) by first exposing the overt deviance of idol worshippers, and then the theoretical deviance of star worshippers. Abraham tells his father Azar that worshipping idols is manifest misguidance. Allah shows Abraham the dominion (malakut) of the heavens and the earth, granting him firm conviction.\n\nAbraham sees a star in the darkness of the night and says, 'This is my Lord'; when the star sets, he says, 'I do not love those that set,' explaining that a star cannot be a deity. He then observes the moon and the sun in turn, and seeing that they also set, he proclaims the belief in Tawhid, saying, 'I have turned my face toward the One who created the heavens and the earth, and I am not of the polytheists.' This event demonstrates that without Allah's guidance, man can fall into misguidance.\n\nHis people begin to argue with Abraham. Abraham says, 'Allah has guided me to the right path; why do you argue with me concerning Him? I do not fear those you associate with Him, unless my Lord wills something. You do not fear associating partners with Allah, so why should I fear?' and adds: 'Those who believe and do not mix their faith with injustice—they are the ones who are in security, and they are rightly guided.'\n\nFollowing these proofs given to Abraham, Allah exalts him. He then enumerates prophets such as Noah, David, Solomon, Job, Joseph, Moses, Aaron, Zechariah, John, Jesus, Elijah, Ishmael, Elisha, Jonah, and Lot. These are the chosen servants whom Allah guided. Even if the Meccan polytheists disbelieve in these prophets, Allah will bring forth another people who will not deny them. Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) is also commanded to follow the path of these prophets.\n\nThe polytheists display great ignorance by saying, 'Allah has not sent down anything to human beings.' Yet the Torah given to Moses is light and guidance for people. They, however, turn the Torah into separate sheets of paper, disclosing some of it and concealing most of it. They do not accept that Allah has sent down scripture. When 'Allah' is mentioned to them, they continue to amuse themselves with idle talk.\n\nThis Book (the Quran) is a blessed book revealed to confirm the scriptures that came before it and to warn the people of Mecca and all humanity around it. Those who believe in the Hereafter believe in this Book and remain steadfast in their prayers. The wisdom behind the revelation of the Quran is not exclusive to the Arabs; while the expression 'Umm al-Qura' points to Mecca as the center, the phrase 'and those around it' targets all of mankind.\n\nThose who did not appraise Allah with true appraisal denied divine revelation by saying, 'Allah has not sent down anything to human beings.' Among those who uttered this statement was Malik ibn al-Sayf, the chief rabbi of the Jews of Medina. When the Messenger of Allah spoke with him and questioned him by adjuring him by the Torah, Malik grew angry and made this statement. The Jews subsequently deposed him from leadership and replaced him with Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf. This event has been narrated as the occasion of revelation (sabab al-nuzul) of the verse.\n\nWho could be more unjust than one who invents a lie against Allah, or says, 'It has been revealed to me,' when nothing has been revealed to him, or claims, 'I will reveal the like of what Allah has sent down'? If only you could see their state while in the agonies of death! The angels stretch forth their hands, saying: 'Surrender your souls. Today you will be recompensed with a humiliating punishment because you used to say wrongful things against Allah and acted arrogantly toward His verses.' On that day, they come all alone just as they were first created, leaving behind everything they possessed; and the partners they trusted in have vanished from them.\n\nUndoubtedly, it is Allah who cleaves the grain and the fruit-stone. He brings forth the living from the dead, and He is the bringer forth of the dead from the living. Such is Allah; how then are you turned away? It is He who cleaves the darkness to bring forth the dawn. He made the night for rest, and the sun and the moon for calculating time. That is the determination of Allah, the All-Mighty, the All-Knowing. A seed or a stone splits beneath the earth and sprouts; this is a clear proof of Allah's power.\n\nIt is He who created the stars for you so that you may guide yourselves by them through the darknesses of the land and the sea. Indeed, We have explained the verses in detail for a people who know. It is He who created you from a single soul; then there is for you a place of dwelling and a place of repository. It is He who sends down water from the sky; with it He brings forth vegetation of every kind, and from that plant He brings forth green foliage, from which He produces grains clustered over one another, clusters of date palms, vineyards, and groves of olives and pomegranates. Look at their fruits when they bear fruit and ripen! Surely in these are lessons for a people who believe. All these signs of creation demonstrate Allah's oneness and power.\n\nThey also made the jinn partners with Allah, even though He created them. Without any knowledge, they fabricated sons and daughters for Him. Exalted and transcendent is His glory far above the attributes they ascribe. He is the Originator of the heavens and the earth out of nothing. How could He have a child when He has no spouse? He is the Creator of all things, and He is the Knower of all things.\n\nAllah created human beings from a single soul, placing some in a place of settlement (settled in the womb) and some in a repository (still in the loins). This difference demonstrates the diversity and variation of creation. The emergence of many different beings from a single source runs contrary to the uniform law of nature, and this is possible only through the power of the Creator. This state of affairs is an evident artistry that points to Allah's existence, knowledge, and power.\n\nThese verses have been explained for those who possess fiqh—that is, people endowed with subtle and profound understanding. For objective proofs are known through subjective proofs. A person's knowing oneself is an essential condition for understanding these verses. Explanations given to those who do not know their own souls are in vain. Those with deep insight grasp the principle in these verses and become privy to the secrets of creation.\n\nAllah sent down water from the sky, and with it He brought forth the vegetation of everything. From it He produced greenery, closely clustered grains (ears of grain), date clusters, vineyards, and fruits such as olives and pomegranates that are both similar and diverse. People ought to understand Allah's power by observing the development of these plants from their unripe state to their ripening. These are manifest proofs for people who believe.\n\nEach of the mentioned fruits has distinct benefits. The date serves as a staple food; grapes are the sultan of fruits; oil is extracted from olives; and the pomegranate, with its hard rind, inner membranes, seeds, and sweet juice, brings contraries together. This diversity and harmony are clear examples of Allah's art of creation.\n\nDespite all these proofs, people associated the jinn as partners with Allah. The concept of jinn encompasses angels, devils, and unseen beings. Some worshipped angels, while others attributed good and evil to two separate deities. Dualist beliefs, such as the Zoroastrians (Majus), fell into shirk by attributing good to Allah and evil to Satan or Ahriman. In truth, both good and evil exist by Allah's creation; there is no creator or deity besides Him.\n\nIn this verse, it is recounted that those who associate partners with Allah took partners from among the jinn. The polytheists worshipped the jinn by considering them partners with Allah, whereas it is Allah who created the jinn as well. This shows that associating the created with the Creator is immense ignorance and injustice. Moreover, this associating of partners encompasses not only idol worship, but also the worship of hidden things such as unseen forces and powers of nature.\n\nSome polytheists attributed sons and daughters to Allah. The Arab polytheists considered the angels to be daughters of Allah, the Zoroastrians regarded Hormizd and Ahriman as sons, the Jews accepted Ezra (Uzayr) as the son of Allah, and the Christians accepted the Messiah as the son of Allah. All of these claims rest on no knowledge whatsoever and are nothing but slander. Allah is transcendent above them; His Essence is far removed from imperfections such as partnership and having children.\n\nHow could Allah have a child when He is the incomparable Originator of the heavens and the earth? He has no spouse, and a child requires a mother. Yet it is impossible for Allah to have a spouse. Birth and giving birth can only be conceived among created beings; such attributes are inconceivable for the Creator. Allah is the Creator of all things and is in need of nothing.\n\nIbda' means creating without a preexisting model. The heavens and the earth were created in this manner. Allah is the unique Creator who brings everything into existence out of nothing. His creation does not depend on matter or form. Material causes come after Allah's creation. He creates as He wills, and nothing can be equal to Him. Allah's knowledge encompasses all things; He knows everything.\n\nSuch is Allah, your Lord. There is no god but He, the Creator of all things. Therefore, worship Him alone and put your trust in Him. He is the Trustee (Wakil) over all things. Eyes cannot perceive Him, but He perceives all eyes. He is the Subtle (al-Latif), the All-Aware (al-Khabir). After these explanations, the truth has now become evident: whoever sees the truth does so to his own benefit, and whoever acts blindly does so to his own detriment.\n\nAllah Almighty informs that eyes cannot perceive Him, yet He perceives all things. Basirah (insight) means seeing with the eye of the heart and attaining true knowledge. Whoever looks with insight and believes does so to his own benefit, and whoever acts blindly does so to his own detriment. Real blindness is not blindness of the eyes, but blindness of the heart. Allah does not protect those who turn blind to proofs of insight; He leaves them in darkness.\n\nAllah explains the verses in diverse ways so that the disbelievers may say, 'You have studied.' Yet these verses are sources of insight for people of knowledge. The Prophet is commanded to follow what is revealed to him from his Lord and pay no heed to the polytheists. Had Allah willed, He could have compelled them to faith, but because He did not so will, they remained polytheists. The Prophet is not a guardian over them.\n\nBelievers must not insult those whom the polytheists worship, lest they revile Allah out of ignorance. To each community their deeds have been made alluring. In the end, the return is to Allah. The polytheists swear that if a miracle comes to them, they will surely believe. Say: Miracles are only with Allah. Allah turns their hearts and their eyes; just as they did not believe at first, so too will they not believe.\n\nEven if angels were sent down to them, the dead spoke to them, and all things were gathered and brought before them, they would not believe unless Allah willed. And thus We have appointed for every prophet enemies—human and jinn devils. They whisper gilded speech to one another in order to deceive. This is so that the hearts of those who do not believe in the Hereafter may incline toward such speech, be pleased with it, and continue committing the offenses they commit.\n\nWhen Allah has sent down the Book fully explained, it is not right to seek a judge other than Him. The word of your Lord has been fulfilled in truth and in justice; there is no one who can alter His words. If you obey most of those on earth, they will lead you astray from the way of Allah; for they follow nothing but conjecture and they do nothing but guess. Your Lord knows best who is guided on the right path.\n\nAllah has sent down to you a Book fully explained in such a way that leaves no need for other miracles, and He has made His judgment definitive. The miracle of this Book is clearer, more comprehensive, and more enduring than other miracles. For instance, while the miracle of the splitting of the moon was a transient and indirect proof, the Quran expresses the same truth clearly and perpetually. For this reason, while Allah's judgment stands, it is not right to incline toward the gilded words of devils and seek another judge.\n\nJust as the Quraysh polytheists turned to soothsayers in their disputes, they also proposed to the Messenger of Allah that he choose an arbiter from among the Jewish or Christian scholars. Allah, however, rejects this proposal by stating: \"Shall I seek a judge other than Allah?\" For the Quran is entirely truthful in its accounts and completely just in its rulings; there is no power that can change it. Allah hears every word and knows every intention, which is why His judgment can neither be overturned nor escaped.\n\nIf you obey the majority of those on earth, they will lead you astray from the path of Allah. For they follow conjecture and their own desires; they do not rely on knowledge. They claim that Allah has revealed nothing, associate partners with Him, and determine what is lawful (halal) and unlawful (haram) according to their whims. Yet Allah knows best who is rightly guided and who strays from the path.\n\nIf you believe in the verses of Allah, eat of that over which the name of Allah has been mentioned. Allah has explained to you what is forbidden to you, except in cases of necessity. Many mislead people without knowledge. Abandon open sin and secret sin; those who earn sin will receive their punishment. Do not eat of that over which the name of Allah has not been mentioned, for this is transgression (fisq). The devils inspire their allies; if you obey them, you will surely become polytheists.\n\nScholars have expressed differing opinions regarding the ruling of the Basmalah in slaughter. Ata stated that the Basmalah is a condition for all kinds of food, but the majority (jumhur) hold the view that this ruling applies only to animals to be slaughtered. If the Basmalah is deliberately omitted, the meat is not eaten; if omitted forgetfully, it may be eaten. According to Imam al-A'zam, if it is deliberately omitted, it is not eaten; if forgotten, it is eaten. Imam Shafi'i, on the other hand, stated that if the one slaughtering is a Muslim or of the People of the Book, what is slaughtered without the Basmalah is lawful (halal). The command in the verses expresses remembering Allah in an absolute sense; the Basmalah is Sunnah.\n\nOne whom We brought to life with guidance when he was dead, and to whom We gave a light with which to walk among people, is not like one who remains in darkness and cannot emerge from it. Thus, what they did has been made pleasing to the disbelievers. In every land, We made its prominent figures criminals so that they might plot deceit therein; yet they only bring evil upon themselves. When a verse comes to them, they say, \"We will not believe unless we too are given prophethood.\" Allah knows best to whom He will give prophethood. Humiliation and severe punishment will reach the criminals.\n\nWhomever Allah wills to guide, He opens his chest to Islam; and whomever He wills to lead astray, He makes his chest tight and constricted, as if he were climbing into the sky. This is the straight path of your Lord. We have explained the verses for a people who take heed. For them is the abode of peace with their Lord; and Allah is their ally because of the good deeds they used to do.\n\nAllah opens the heart of the one He wills to guide to Islam and places such a light in his heart that his chest expands and finds relief through faith and obedience. When the Prophet (peace be upon him) was asked about the sign of this opening, he said, 'Turning toward the home of eternity, turning away from the abode of delusion, and preparing for death before it arrives.' And whomever Allah wills to lead astray, He constricts his chest, suffocating him as if he were climbing into the sky; thus the torment and defilement of disbelief are placed upon him. This path is the straight path of your Lord, and all signs have been explained in the Quran for those who reflect.\n\nOn the day Allah gathers humans and jinn, He says to the jinn, 'O company of jinn! You have led many humans astray.' Those among humans who were allies of the jinn will say, 'Our Lord! We benefited from one another, and we have reached our appointed term that You set for us.' Allah will say, 'Your dwelling place is the Fire, where you will abide eternally, except as Allah wills.' Surely your Lord is All-Wise, All-Knowing. Thus We make the wrongdoers allies of one another because of the sins they used to earn.\n\nWhen Allah says, 'O company of jinn and humans! Did there not come to you messengers from among you, reciting My verses to you and warning you of the meeting of this Day of yours?' they will say, 'We bear witness against ourselves.' The worldly life deceived them, and they bore witness against themselves that they were disbelievers. In this scene, the human allies of the jinn attempt to excuse the jinn by assuming responsibility, yet all of them deserve the same sin and punishment.\n\nThe polytheists used to allocate a share for Allah from the crops and livestock He created, and also a share for their partners (idols). What belonged to their partners would not reach Allah, whereas what belonged to Allah would reach their partners. Likewise, their partners made the killing of their children appear pleasing to many of the polytheists; they wanted them both to ruin themselves and to confuse and corrupt their religion. Had Allah willed, they would not have done so. So leave them with what they fabricate.\n\nThe polytheists said, according to their own claims, 'These livestock and crops are forbidden; none may eat of them except whom we will.' They prohibited riding on the backs of some animals, and slaughtered others without mentioning the name of Allah. As for what was in the wombs of the livestock, they said, 'It is lawful only for our males and forbidden to our females; but if it is born dead, then they all share in it.' All of these were rulings made by fabricating lies against Allah. Allah has caused those who killed their children out of ignorance and forbade the lawful sustenance to suffer loss; they have strayed from the right path.\n\nAllah created gardens, both trellised and untrellised, date palms, crops, olives, and pomegranates. Eat of their fruit when they bear fruit, give its due (its zakat and charity) on the day of harvest, and do not be wasteful. Of the livestock, He created those that carry burdens and those used for furnishings. Eat of the sustenance that Allah has provided, and do not follow the footsteps of Satan. He created eight pairs: sheep, goats, camels, and cattle. Who can be more unjust than one who invents a lie against Allah regarding prohibitions? In what was revealed to our Prophet, the only things made forbidden are carrion, spilled blood, the flesh of swine, and animals slaughtered in the name of other than Allah. Whoever is forced by necessity, without transgressing or exceeding limits, may eat of them. Allah is Most Forgiving, Most Merciful. For the Jews, animals with claws and certain fats were made forbidden. Say to the polytheists: their claim that 'Had Allah willed, we would not have associated partners with Him, nor would we have forbidden anything' is the word of those before them. The most decisive argument belongs to Allah; had He willed, He would have guided you all. Do not follow the whims of those who deny My verses and do not believe in the Hereafter.\n\nThe Arab polytheists used to declare certain animals forbidden by inventing lies against Allah. When the rulings of Islam began to be revealed, they entered into a dispute with the Messenger of Allah. According to a narration, their orator, Malik ibn Awf, came to the Messenger of Allah and said, 'You are making certain things lawful.' The Messenger of Allah asked, 'You made things forbidden without basis; where did you get the idea to forbid them when the eight pairs created by Allah are lawful?' When Malik was unable to answer, he fell silent. Thereupon, Allah explained the eight pairs He created and made lawful.\n\nThe expression 'eight pairs' mentioned in the verse means four pairs—male and female—of sheep, goat, camel, and cattle species, that is, eight individuals. In these verses, a methodology regarding declaring things forbidden is demonstrated: first, eight pairs are enumerated, then grouped into four species; subsequently, attributes that might potentially be considered grounds for prohibition—such as being male, being female, or being in the womb—are specified in detail, and it is proven that none of these has any relevance to prohibition. This is the finest example of the method called 'takhrij and tanqih al-manat' (derivation and refinement of the ratio legis) in Islamic jurisprudence (fiqh). However, this rational method alone is not sufficient; the effective cause (illah) must be based upon an established foundation, which can only be established through revelation.\n\nThereupon it is stated: Say, I do not find in what has been revealed to me anything forbidden to someone who would eat it, except carrion, spilled blood, the flesh of swine—for it is impure—and animals slaughtered in the name of other than Allah are forbidden. The concept of impurity (rijs) is not limited to external appearance alone; things that are spiritually impure, such as alcohol, are also forbidden. Moreover, 'transgression' (fisq)—that is, what is slaughtered by invoking a name other than Allah—is also forbidden. One who is compelled by necessity, without violating the rights of others and without exceeding the required amount, may eat of them. These prohibitions can be extended on grounds such as aggression and excessiveness; indeed, predatory beasts and impure insects have also been forbidden on these principles.\n\nEarlier, We also forbade every animal with claws to the Jews. The term claw (zufur) encompasses various animals such as camels, horses, and beasts of prey; however, cattle and sheep are outside this scope. Furthermore, We forbade the internal fats of cattle and sheep to them, except for the fats adhering to the back, the entrails, or mixed with bone (such as tail fat), which were not forbidden. This prohibition was a punishment due to the aggression of the Jews. Allah speaks the truth; this prohibition came about because of their wrongdoing.\n\nThe polytheists use destiny as an excuse, saying, 'If Allah had willed, neither we nor our forefathers would have associated partners with Him, nor would we have forbidden anything.' However, while Allah's will encompasses everything, this does not mean that what they did was right. Allah decrees sin, but does not approve of it; He has given human beings freedom of choice and responsibility. Past nations also denied their prophets and in the end tasted the punishment. Therefore, these polytheists will suffer the same fate. Allah's mercy is vast, but the sinners will sooner or later meet their punishment.\n\nThe polytheists were trying to defend themselves by saying, \"If Allah had willed, we would not have associated partners with Him, nor would we have forbidden anything.\" Yet this claim of theirs is based not on knowledge, but merely on conjecture and delusion. By perpetuating the lies and slander inherited from their forefathers, they fabricate lies against Allah. Following their own whims and desires, they distort the truth and ignore the proofs of Allah. In reality, they are only lying and deceiving themselves.\n\nO Muhammad! Say: The most decisive and conclusive proof belongs to Allah. Allah has created countless signs in the heavens and the earth, provided lessons from past communities, given you intellect and will, and sent scriptures and prophets. Although this claim of yours is an attempt to defend yourselves by using Allah's will, it is actually a proof entirely against you. For by saying, \"If Allah had willed, we could not have done it,\" you confess that you cannot oppose His will, but then with this confession you attempt to justify your polytheism (shirk). This is a contradiction and in fact proves tawhid (monotheism).\n\nO Muhammad! Say to the polytheists: Bring forth your witnesses who will testify that Allah made this forbidden. If they come and testify, \"Allah forbade these,\" do not testify with them. Do not follow the desires of those who deny Our verses, who do not believe in the Hereafter, and who associate partners with Allah. Their testimony is nothing other than false testimony. This verse, returning to the beginning of the surah, emphasizes the truth of tawhid and prophethood.\n\nSay: Come, let me recite what your Lord has forbidden to you: Do not associate anything with Him. Treat parents with kindness. Do not kill your children out of fear of poverty; We provide sustenance for you and for them. Do not approach immoralities, whether open or secret. Do not take a life that Allah has made sacred, except by right. These are what Allah has enjoined upon you. Also, do not approach the property of an orphan, except in the fairest manner. Give full measure and weight with justice; We do not burden any soul beyond its capacity. Whenever you speak, be just, even if it concerns a close relative. Fulfill the covenant of Allah. These are what He has enjoined upon you so that you may take heed.\n\nAnd this is My straight path, so follow it. Do not follow other paths that will separate you from the path of Allah. Allah has enjoined these upon you so that you may be mindful. These verses resemble the Ten Commandments in the Torah and are decisive rulings common to all divine laws. Whoever obeys these commands and avoids the prohibitions attains salvation; whoever turns away from them falls into misguidance. The path of the Prophet is the path of tawhid; uniting on this path is a divine strength. Those who fall into division will be ruined.\n\nAllah Almighty informs that He gave the Torah to Moses (Musa) and that this scripture was a guidance and mercy explaining all things. Then, it is emphasized that this Quran is also a blessed scripture, that it must be followed, and that one must fear Allah. The Quran was sent down to eliminate excuses and pretexts such as that the previous scriptures were sent down to only two communities (Jews and Christians) while we were unaware, or that had a scripture been given to us, we would have been better guided.\n\nThe fact that this scripture was sent down in Arabic also invalidates excuses such as the Arab society's illiteracy. For every community that hears that the book has been revealed is obligated to learn it and spread it. On the Day of Resurrection, such excuses will not be accepted.\n\nThe disbelievers wait for the angels, your Lord, or some of His signs (portents) to come. However, on the day when the signs of your Lord arrive, faith on that day will not benefit anyone who did not believe before or who did not earn any good through their faith. Elmalılı explains here that there are two kinds of signs: one consists of signs foretelling future events (faith must be placed in these), and the other consists of signs where events actually occur, at which point faith is of no benefit. He details the prerequisite of prior faith and earning good for faith to be accepted, and touches upon the view of the Mu'tazilah.\n\nIn verse 159, it is stated that the Prophet has no relation to those who divided their religion and broke up into sects. Among these groups, polytheists, Jews, Christians, and even those who broke away from the Muslims can be counted. Our Prophet reported that his ummah would split into seventy-three sects, and that only one would be saved. In verse 160, it is stated that whoever brings a good deed will receive tenfold reward, while whoever brings an evil deed will be penalized only with its equal. This is a manifestation of Allah's justice and grace. The punishment for disbelief (kufr) is eternal, because disbelief is an eternal evil.\n\nIn verses 161–163, our Prophet is commanded to say that he has been guided to a straight path, the upright (hanif) religion of Abraham, that his prayer, his acts of worship, his life, and his death are for Allah, the Lord of the worlds, that He has no partner, and that he is the first of the Muslims. Finally, in verses 164–165, it is emphasized that while Allah is the Lord of all things, seeking a Lord other than Him is meaningless, that whatever each person earns belongs to them alone, and that no one will bear the burden of another. Allah has made human beings successors upon the earth and tests them through the blessings He has bestowed upon them.\n\nWith His decree, \"Indeed, I will place a vicegerent upon the earth\" (al-Baqarah, 2:30), Almighty Allah also granted this ummah the high station and honor He gave to Adam, making them successors upon the earth. This responsibility and trust have been placed upon humankind.\n\nAllah has raised some people above others by degrees in attributes such as intellect, knowledge, honor, rank, wealth, and provision. The purpose of this is to test human beings, to distinguish those who use the granted blessings properly and show gratitude from those who do not, and to select those who do the best deeds.\n\nThis world is a realm of trial and competition. Today's condition is the result of the past trial, and tomorrow's condition will be the result of today's trial. The Ummah of Muhammad is in a test not only among its own individuals, but also alongside the nations that came before it, and must take heed from them and win the race.\n\nThe variation in degrees is a requirement of this test and competition. For this reason, those who are at the top may fall, and those who are below may rise. The danger and responsibility of those occupying higher ranks are heavier. Therefore, one should not take pride in high worldly status and rank, but should strive as is due.\n\nO Muhammad! Your Lord's punishment is swift. To the disbelievers and rebels who do not fulfill the due of the blessings He has bestowed and do not show gratitude—no matter how high a station they may hold—your Lord brings about their affliction at whichever moment He wills. Everything that is to come is near. Along with this, Allah is All-Forgiving and Most Merciful; He overlooks the shortcomings of those who are grateful and obedient, and brings them into His mercy.\n\nIf the time when this surah was revealed in Mecca and the subsequent stages of Islamic history are considered, it becomes evident how many miracles concerning the future this verse contains, and that this still continues. With the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) being sent as a messenger, a new era in human life opened that would surpass past communities as well.\n\nSurah al-An'am ends here. Now, let us listen to Surah al-A'raf, which explains how the appointment as successors and the trial mentioned in this verse have unfolded since the past."